# KNOWN GAP (R42) — `gg fmt` SILENTLY DELETES a line the author wrote.
#
# MUST EXCLUDE from any bulk `gg fmt` sweep: this fixture's SUBJECT is what
# `gg fmt` does to an attribute line, so sweeping it bakes the defect in.
#
# An attribute above a construct whose parser arm DISCARDS attributes never
# reaches the AST, and the formatter prints from the AST — so the line is gone
# from the output. Exit 0, no diagnostic; with `gg fmt --in-place` the source
# is lost from disk. Core #10 (lower-or-reject) applied to the formatter: it
# must preserve the line or refuse to format, never quietly drop it.
#
# Measured at HEAD (2026-08-17), `gg fmt` on this file:
#     attribute lines in : 5
#     attribute lines out: 1        (only the one above a FUNCTION survives)
#     exit 0, no diagnostic of any kind
#
# The surviving `keeps_it` costume is the CONTROL and the discriminator: the
# `function` arm stores attributes, so the same spelling round-trips there.
# The other four sample four cells of the dropping-position axis (static,
# type alias, newtype, const decl). They are a SAMPLE, not the axis — the
# full position set is larger, and pinning it is the class guard's job.
#
# INTENDED: an unknown attribute is REJECTED (`E_UnknownDirective`), so
# `gg fmt` refuses to format exactly as it already does for a parse error
# ("N parse error(s) found; refusing to format", exit 1). A KNOWN attribute in
# these positions is HONOURED and round-trips — attributes attach to
# DECLARATIONS, and every construct here introduces a name (ratified
# 2026-08-17, docs/define-gorget/decisions.md).
#
# Wired test: fmt_does_not_delete_attribute_lines (tests/integration.rs,
# #[ignore]d). It asserts every attribute line SURVIVES, so it is RED at HEAD
# and goes green when the positions honour or reject. Graduate it out of
# known_gaps/ the round that lands.
@bogusattr
void keeps_it():
    print(1)

@bogusattr
public static int S = 7

@bogusattr
type Alias = int

@bogusattr
newtype Meters(int)

@bogusattr
const int C = 3
